  "nameStory": "The White-banded Sweat Bee gets its name from the distinctive pale bands that ring its dark abdomen, creating a striking striped pattern. Like other sweat bees, it earned the 'sweat' part of its name because these small bees are attracted to human perspiration, which they sip for its valuable salt content.",
  "description": "The White-banded Sweat Bee is a small, solitary bee about the size of a housefly, with a dark body decorated by prominent white or pale yellow bands across its abdomen. Unlike honeybees that live in large colonies, each female builds her own nest and raises her young alone. These industrious little bees are excellent pollinators, visiting a wide variety of flowers throughout the growing season. Despite their small size, they're quite noticeable when they land on your arm on a warm day to sample your salty sweat.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White bands",
      "description": "Look for distinct white or pale yellow bands that wrap around the dark abdomen like tiny belts - these bands are crisp and clearly defined, not fuzzy or broken",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and build",
      "description": "About half the size of a honeybee with a compact, sturdy build - they feel surprisingly solid if one lands on you",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Quick, darting flight with frequent hovering near flowers - they move with jerky, purposeful movements rather than smooth gliding",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Behavior",
      "description": "Often attracted to human sweat and will gently land on skin - they feel ticklish but rarely sting unless directly handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Head and thorax",
      "description": "Dark metallic head and thorax that may show subtle green or bronze hints in bright sunlight, with large prominent eyes",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Legs",
      "description": "Dark legs that often carry visible pollen loads as fuzzy, colorful bundles on their hind legs",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for White-banded Sweat Bees in gardens, meadows, parks, and forest edges throughout New England where diverse flowers bloom. They nest in the ground, often in sandy or well-drained soil along paths, in bare patches of gardens, or on sunny slopes. You'll often spot them visiting flowers in both wild and cultivated areas, from backyard flower beds to roadside wildflower patches.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults begin emerging in late spring when early flowers like dandelions and fruit trees are blooming. Watch for them on warm, sunny days visiting spring wildflowers.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when they're most commonly seen visiting a wide variety of flowers and occasionally landing on people to sample sweat. This is the best time to observe their distinctive white bands and busy foraging behavior.",
    "fall": "Activity continues into early fall as they visit late-season flowers like asters and goldenrod, building up reserves before winter. Numbers gradually decline as temperatures drop.",
    "winter": "Adults overwinter in protected locations underground or in plant debris, completely inactive and not observable until spring warmth returns."
  },
  "funFact": "This little bee is actually a world traveler that somehow made it from Europe to North America, and genetic studies reveal that the entire North American population descended from just one single female bee who made the journey - making every White-banded Sweat Bee in New England part of one enormous family!",
  "confusionNotes": "White-banded Sweat Bees can be confused with other small dark bees, but their crisp white abdominal bands are the key - many similar bees have fuzzy hair bands or less distinct markings. Remember: if it's small, dark, has sharp white bands (not fuzzy), and seems interested in your sweat, it's likely this species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The scientific name Lasioglossum leucozonium was once known as Lasioglossum similis, so you might see both names in older field guides. This widespread species demonstrates how even tiny insects can disperse across continents, though exactly how that founding female made it across the Atlantic Ocean remains a mystery.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's many Lasioglossum sweat bees, the White-banded Sweat Bee is distinguished by its particularly crisp, bright white abdominal bands. While the Deep-blue Sweat Bee has metallic blue coloration and the Black-and-green Metallic Sweat Bee shows green highlights, leucozonium is more plainly colored but with those unmistakable sharp white stripes. Most other sweat bees in the genus have either less distinct banding, different colors, or more metallic sheens.",
